{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070ab-e5ec-77e1-8b81-6518e8a08211"},"sequence":120,"recorded_at":1780531400000235,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34"},"call_id":"call_01a070ac24ee7430be85e4a85bf22fee","text":"Read text file `internal/provhost/runner.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"bytes\"\n5|\t\"context\"\n6|\t\"io\"\n7|\t\"os\"\n8|\t\"os/exec\"\n9|\t\"time\"\n10|)\n11|\n12|// waitDrainDelay bounds how long Wait lingers for pipe EOF after the\n13|// deadline kill before giving up on detached grandchildren.\n14|const waitDrainDelay = 2 * time.Second\n15|\n16|// stdoutCap bounds captured plugin stdout: one maximal frame, its line\n17|// terminator, and one probe byte, so a maximal frame followed by any\n18|// trailing byte is still distinguishable from a clean end. Anything\n19|// beyond is cut; the splitFrame length checks stay exact on the capped\n20|// capture.\n21|const stdoutCap = MaxFrameBytes + 2\n22|\n23|// Result is what a plugin process produced: captured stdout and stderr\n24|// and the process exit code. Stdout and stderr are kept separate end to\n25|// end: diagnostics never enter frame parsing, and frame bytes never\n26|// enter diagnostics. Captures are capped (stdout at one frame plus a\n27|// probe byte, stderr at StderrCapBytes); beyond the cap the stream is cut\n28|// and the failure is decided from what was captured, which the length\n29|// checks keep exact.\n30|type Result struct {\n31|\tStdout   []byte\n32|\tStderr   []byte\n33|\tExitCode int\n34|}\n35|\n36|// Runner starts one plugin process for one operation frame. Production\n37|// code supplies ExecRunner; tests supply a scripted fake. A Runner moves\n38|// bytes and reports process fate; it never parses frames and never\n39|// decides failures.\n40|type Runner interface {\n41|\tRun(ctx context.Context, executable string, stdin []byte) (Result, error)\n42|}\n43|\n44|// ExecRunner is the production Runner: it starts the trusted executable\n45|// discovered by internal/provider, writes exactly one frame to its stdin,\n46|// closes stdin, and waits under the caller's context. Environment and\n47|// stderr content never enter failure human text: Result carries the raw\n48|// streams, and failures built from a Result carry the failure class and\n49|// member names only, never content.\n50|type ExecRunner struct{}\n51|\n52|// Run starts executable with stdin on its stdin and captures both streams\n53|// with caps. A nil error with a nonzero exit code is an ordinary result:\n54|// a syntactically valid frame governs regardless of the exit status,\n55|// because SHOULD exit 0 is not MUST. A non-nil error means no result\n56|// could be judged: start failure, stream failure, context end, or a\n57|// wait failure with empty stdout and no known exit code. In\n58|// particular, a stdin write failure racing the plugin's exit never\n59|// discards a judgeable result on its own.\n60|func (ExecRunner) Run(ctx context.Context, executable string, stdin []byte) (Result, error) {\n61|\tcommand := newCommandContext(ctx, executable)\n62|\tcommand.WaitDelay = waitDrainDelay\n63|\tstdinPipe, err := command.StdinPipe()\n64|\tif err != nil {\n65|\t\treturn Result{}, err\n66|\t}\n67|\t// Stdout and stderr travel over pipes owned here, not over\n68|\t// StdoutPipe/StderrPipe: Wait closes those read ends after seeing\n69|\t// the process exit, which races a drain still in flight and\n70|\t// surfaces as \"file already closed\" on a valid frame. A pipe\n71|\t// created here is closed only by this function, so a drain never\n72|\t// loses to the wait.\n73|\tstdoutReader, stdoutWriter, err := os.Pipe()\n74|\tif err != nil {\n75|\t\treturn Result{}, err\n76|\t}\n77|\tdefer stdoutReader.Close()\n78|\tstderrReader, stderrWriter, err := os.Pipe()\n79|\tif err != nil {\n80|\t\tstdoutReader.Close()\n81|\t\treturn Result{}, err\n82|\t}\n83|\tdefer stderrReader.Close()\n84|\tcommand.Stdout = stdoutWriter\n85|\tcommand.Stderr = stderrWriter\n86|\tif err := command.Start(); err != nil {\n87|\t\tstdoutWriter.Close()\n88|\t\tstderrWriter.Close()\n89|\t\tstdoutReader.Close()\n90|\t\tstderrReader.Close()\n91|\t\treturn Result{}, err\n92|\t}\n93|\t// The parent's copies close at once: the child holds its own, so\n94|\t// EOF still means every writer is gone while no later Close can\n95|\t// interrupt a drain.\n96|\t_ = stdoutWriter.Close()\n97|\t_ = stderrWriter.Close()\n98|\t// Drain both streams while stdin is written: a plugin that answers\n99|\t// without reading stdin would otherwise deadlock against a full\n100|\t// pipe once either direction exceeds its buffer.\n101|\ttype streamResult struct {\n102|\t\tdata []byte\n103|\t\terr  error\n104|\t}\n105|\tstdoutDone := make(chan streamResult, 1)\n106|\tstderrDone := make(chan streamResult, 1)\n107|\tgo func() {\n108|\t\tdata, err := readCapped(stdoutReader, stdoutCap)\n109|\t\tstdoutDone <- streamResult{data: data, err: err}\n110|\t}()\n111|\tgo func() {\n112|\t\tdata, err := readCapped(stderrReader, StderrCapBytes)\n113|\t\tstderrDone <- streamResult{data: data, err: err}\n114|\t}()\n115|\twrote, writeErr := stdinPipe.Write(stdin)\n116|\tif writeErr == nil && wrote < len(stdin) {\n117|\t\twriteErr = io.ErrShortWrite\n118|\t}\n119|\tif writeErr == nil {\n120|\t\t_, writeErr = stdinPipe.Write([]byte{'\\n'})\n121|\t}\n122|\tcloseErr := stdinPipe.Close()\n123|\twaitErr := command.Wait()\n124|\tif ctx.Err() != nil {\n125|\t\t// The wait ended by deadline or cancellation: the kill was\n126|\t\t// already signaled, but a detached grandchild may hold the\n127|\t\t// pipes open past WaitDelay. Close them rather than waiting\n128|\t\t// for streams that can no longer decide anything: with the\n129|\t\t// deadline gone there is no valid response to read.\n130|\t\tstdoutReader.Close()\n131|\t\tstderrReader.Close()\n132|\t\t<-stdoutDone\n133|\t\t<-stderrDone\n134|\t\treturn Result{}, ctx.Err()\n135|\t}\n136|\tstdoutResult := <-stdoutDone\n137|\tstderrResult := <-stderrDone\n138|\tif stdoutResult.err != nil {\n139|\t\treturn Result{}, stdoutResult.err\n140|\t}\n141|\tif stderrResult.err != nil {\n142|\t\treturn Result{}, stderrResult.err\n143|\t}\n144|\tstdout := stdoutResult.data\n145|\tstderr := stderrResult.data\n146|\t// A stdin write failure after the process ran is still a result when\n147|\t// the process produced one: the frame may have arrived whole while\n148|\t// the write or close raced the plugin's exit. Report write, close,\n149|\t// and wait failures only when no result can be judged — empty\n150|\t// stdout with no known exit code — otherwise the frame governs,\n151|\t// and an empty stdout with a known exit code classifies the crash\n152|\t// through the returned Result instead of masking it as a transport\n153|\t// failure. Returning writeErr before stdout is ever considered\n154|\t// discards valid correlated frames on an EPIPE race.\n155|\texitCode := -1\n156|\tif command.ProcessState != nil {\n157|\t\texitCode = command.ProcessState.ExitCode()\n158|\t}\n159|\tif exit, ok := waitErr.(*exec.ExitError); ok {\n160|\t\texitCode = exit.ExitCode()\n161|\t}\n162|\tresult := Result{Stdout: stdout, Stderr: stderr, ExitCode: exitCode}\n163|\tif len(stdout) > 0 || command.ProcessState != nil {\n164|\t\treturn result, nil\n165|\t}\n166|\tif writeErr != nil {\n167|\t\treturn Result{}, writeErr\n168|\t}\n169|\tif closeErr != nil {\n170|\t\treturn Result{}, closeErr\n171|\t}\n172|\tif waitErr != nil {\n173|\t\treturn Result{}, waitErr\n174|\t}\n175|\treturn result, nil\n176|}\n177|\n178|// readCapped drains stream up to cap bytes and cuts the rest. Memory\n179|// stays bounded no matter what the plugin emits, and the splitFrame\n180|// length checks stay exact on the capped capture.\n181|func readCapped(stream io.Reader, cap int) ([]byte, error) {\n182|\tdata, err := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(stream, int64(cap)+1))\n183|\tif err != nil {\n184|\t\treturn nil, err\n185|\t}\n186|\tif len(data) > cap {\n187|\t\treturn data[:cap], nil\n188|\t}\n189|\treturn data, nil\n190|}\n191|\n192|// Host invokes provider plugins: one process per operation, one frame in,\n193|// one frame out, under the request deadline. Host keeps no cross-call\n194|// state, so successive calls are separate processes observing durable\n195|// state through the authority the caller passes.\n196|type Host struct {\n197|\t// Runner starts the plugin process. It must be set; a nil Runner is\n198|\t// a caller error, refused before any frame is built.\n199|\tRunner Runner\n200|\t// Now is the clock deadlines are checked against. Nil means the\n201|\t// system clock; tests supply a fixed instant.\n202|\tNow func() time.Time\n203|}\n204|\n205|func (host Host) now() time.Time {\n206|\tif host.Now == nil {\n207|\t\treturn time.Now()\n208|\t}\n209|\treturn host.Now()\n210|}\n211|\n212|// splitFrame separates the single response line from anything following\n213|// it. oversize reports a first line beyond MaxFrameBytes; extra reports\n214|// any bytes after the first line terminator. An empty line is neither:\n215|// it is a missing response, judged with the exit code by the caller.\n216|//\n217|// The checks are length-only and exact: stdout capture holds at most\n218|// MaxFrameBytes+2 bytes, so a first line beyond the limit, or any\n219|// trailing byte, is always visible in the captured length. No separate\n220|// truncation flag participates; a narrowed or dropped disjunct here\n221|// would be equivalent, not coverage.\n222|func splitFrame(captured []byte) (line []byte, oversize bool, extra bool) {\n223|\tif index := bytes.IndexByte(captured, '\\n'); index >= 0 {\n224|\t\treturn captured[:index], len(captured[:index]) > MaxFrameBytes, index+1 < len(captured)\n225|\t}\n226|\treturn captured, len(captured) > MaxFrameBytes, false\n227|}\n228|\n229|// Call dispatches one operation: it frames the request, starts one plugin\n230|// process, enforces the request deadline, and interprets the single\n231|// response frame. Encode failures return before the Runner is touched, so\n232|// an unknown operation never starts a process. A success envelope returns\n233|// its body; a failure envelope returns the bound child error; anything\n234|// else returns a local failure that trusts nothing from the plugin.\n235|//\n236|// Deadline accounting is exact: the wait ends under a context cut at the\n237|// request deadline, and provider_timeout is reported only when that\n238|// instant actually passed. A parent cancellation first, or any other\n239|// runner failure with time remaining, is provider_process_failed. An\n240|// empty stdout with time remaining is a missing response:\n241|// provider_protocol_error on exit 0, provider_process_failed otherwise.\n242|// An empty stdout after the deadline passed is provider_timeout, and a\n243|// valid frame governs regardless of lateness or exit status.\n244|func (host Host) Call(ctx context.Context, executable string, req Request) (Response, error) {\n245|\tif host.Runner == nil {\n246|\t\tfailure, err := failInvalid(\"host has no runner\")\n247|\t\tif err != nil {\n248|\t\t\treturn Response{}, err\n249|\t\t}\n250|\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n251|\t}\n252|\tframe, deadline, err := encodeFrame(req, host.now())\n253|\tif err != nil {\n254|\t\treturn Response{}, err\n255|\t}\n256|\tdeadlineCtx, cancel := context.WithDeadline(ctx, deadline)\n257|\tdefer cancel()\n258|\tresult, err := host.Runner.Run(deadlineCtx, executable, frame)\n259|\tif err != nil {\n260|\t\tnow := host.now()\n261|\t\tif !now.Before(deadline) || deadlineCtx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n262|\t\t\tfailure, fault := failTimeout(\"no response before the request deadline\", deadline.UnixMilli())\n263|\t\t\tif fault != nil {\n264|\t\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n265|\t\t\t}\n266|\t\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n267|\t\t}\n268|\t\tfailure, fault := failProcess(\"runner reported no result\", err)\n269|\t\tif fault != nil {\n270|\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n271|\t\t}\n272|\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n273|\t}\n274|\tline, oversize, extra := splitFrame(result.Stdout)\n275|\tif oversize {\n276|\t\tfailure, fault := failProtocol(\"frame exceeds 8 MiB\", \"\")\n277|\t\tif fault != nil {\n278|\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n279|\t\t}\n280|\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n281|\t}\n282|\tif extra {\n283|\t\tfailure, fault := failProtocol(\"stdout carries more than one frame\", \"\")\n284|\t\tif fault != nil {\n285|\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n286|\t\t}\n287|\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n288|\t}\n289|\tif len(line) == 0 {\n290|\t\t// No valid response arrived. When the request deadline passed,\n291|\t\t// this is the timeout even if the process also exited badly:\n292|\t\t// the host waited the full allowance and got nothing usable.\n293|\t\tif !host.now().Before(deadline) || deadlineCtx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n294|\t\t\tfailure, fault := failTimeout(\"no response before the request deadline\", deadline.UnixMilli())\n295|\t\t\tif fault != nil {\n296|\t\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n297|\t\t\t}\n298|\t\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n299|\t\t}\n300|\t\tif result.ExitCode != 0 {\n301|\t\t\tfailure, fault := failProcess(\"plugin exited without a response\", nil)\n302|\t\t\tif fault != nil {\n303|\t\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n304|\t\t\t}\n305|\t\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n306|\t\t}\n307|\t\tfailure, fault := failProtocol(\"plugin exited without a response\", \"\")\n308|\t\tif fault != nil {\n309|\t\t\treturn Response{}, fault\n310|\t\t}\n311|\t\treturn Response{}, failure\n312|\t}\n313|\treturn DecodeResponse(line, req.RequestID)\n314|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070ab-e5ec-77e1-8b81-6518e8a08211"},"sequence":121,"recorded_at":1780531400000237,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34"},"call_id":"call_01a070ac258a72b08083c456db1ebfc8","text":"Read text file `internal/provhost/surrogate.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|// This file is the local lone-surrogate gate for the Section 1.6 wire\n4|// bodies this package decodes. Pinned specification Section 1.6 requires\n5|// decoders to reject lone surrogate code points before canonicalization,\n6|// and the repository already enforces that rule in\n7|// internal/canonicaljson (validateSurrogateEscapes, called from\n8|// decodeStrict). This package decodes with its own strict object reader\n9|// (decodeStrictObject in protocol.go) because it must keep member-level\n10|// fault attribution (duplicate member names, the offending member) that\n11|// the shared decoder does not report, and encoding/json silently replaces\n12|// a lone \\ud800-style escape with U+FFFD instead of failing. So the gate\n13|// lives here, scans the raw frame bytes before decoding, and refuses the\n14|// same class the shared package refuses.\n15|//\n16|// The gate is deliberately a copy, not an import: internal/canonicaljson\n17|// is byte-identical shared baseline text that two unlanded stories must\n18|// not edit at once, so neither story may add an export there. The\n19|// surrogate-vector pin test in surrogate_test.go drives both this gate\n20|// (through decodeStrictObject) and canonicaljson.Canonicalize over one\n21|// shared corpus, so the copies cannot drift silently: a divergence\n22|// reddens there rather than passing unnoticed.\n23|//\n24|// The gate decides the surrogate question only. Malformed escapes,\n25|// unterminated strings, and non-UTF-8 bytes are not its verdict: they\n26|// fall through to the JSON-syntax arms that already own them. A lone\n27|// high surrogate is a high unit not immediately followed by a \\uDC00–\n28|// \\uDFFF escape; a lone low surrogate is a low unit in any other\n29|// position. Both are refused before any member is read.\n30|\n31|// hasLoneSurrogateEscape reports whether raw JSON bytes carry a lone\n32|// surrogate escape inside any string. The string walk mirrors the shared\n33|// package's walker (backslash handling included: a \\\\ escape consumes\n34|// both bytes, so the text \"\\\\ud800\" is backslash-plus-text, not an\n35|// escape), but only the surrogate verdict is returned.\n36|func hasLoneSurrogateEscape(input []byte) bool {\n37|\tfor index := 0; index < len(input); index++ {\n38|\t\tif input[index] != '\"' {\n39|\t\t\tcontinue\n40|\t\t}\n41|\t\tfor index++; index < len(input); index++ {\n42|\t\t\tif input[index] == '\"' {\n43|\t\t\t\tgoto stringComplete\n44|\t\t\t}\n45|\t\t\tif input[index] != '\\\\' {\n46|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n47|\t\t\t}\n48|\t\t\tindex++\n49|\t\t\tif index >= len(input) || input[index] != 'u' {\n50|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n51|\t\t\t}\n52|\t\t\tunit, end, ok := readHexUnit(input, index-1)\n53|\t\t\tif !ok {\n54|\t\t\t\t// Not a well-formed \\uXXXX escape: the\n55|\t\t\t\t// JSON-syntax arms own it, not this gate.\n56|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n57|\t\t\t}\n58|\t\t\tindex = end - 1\n59|\t\t\tif unit >= 0xd800 && unit <= 0xdbff {\n60|\t\t\t\tlow, lowEnd, ok := readHexUnit(input, end)\n61|\t\t\t\tif !ok || low < 0xdc00 || low > 0xdfff {\n62|\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n63|\t\t\t\t}\n64|\t\t\t\tindex = lowEnd - 1\n65|\t\t\t} else if unit >= 0xdc00 && unit <= 0xdfff {\n66|\t\t\t\treturn true\n67|\t\t\t}\n68|\t\t}\n69|\t\t// The string never closed: malformed JSON the syntax arms\n70|\t\t// refuse, so the gate claims no surrogate verdict here.\n71|\t\treturn false\n72|\tstringComplete:\n73|\t}\n74|\treturn false\n75|}\n76|\n77|// readHexUnit parses one \\uXXXX escape at input[start]. It reports false\n78|// when the bytes are not a well-formed escape; hex digits parse in\n79|// either case, matching the shared package's ParseUint base-16 read.\n80|func readHexUnit(input []byte, start int) (uint16, int, bool) {\n81|\tif start < 0 || start+6 > len(input) || input[start] != '\\\\' || input[start+1] != 'u' {\n82|\t\treturn 0, start, false\n83|\t}\n84|\tvar value uint16\n85|\tfor _, b := range input[start+2 : start+6] {\n86|\t\tvar digit byte\n87|\t\tif b >= '0' && b <= '9' {\n88|\t\t\tdigit = b - '0'\n89|\t\t} else if b >= 'a' && b <= 'f' {\n90|\t\t\tdigit = b - 'a' + 10\n91|\t\t} else if b >= 'A' && b <= 'F' {\n92|\t\t\tdigit = b - 'A' + 10\n93|\t\t} else {\n94|\t\t\treturn 0, start, false\n95|\t\t}\n96|\t\tvalue = value*16 + uint16(digit)\n97|\t}\n98|\treturn value, start + 6, true\n99|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070ab-e5ec-77e1-8b81-6518e8a08211"},"sequence":122,"recorded_at":1780531400000239,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34"},"call_id":"call_01a070ac261c7b32a3609f1efcd819e9","text":"Read text file `internal/provhost/surrogate_test.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"strings\"\n5|\t\"testing\"\n6|\n7|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/canonicaljson\"\n8|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n9|)\n10|\n11|// TestProductionEntriesRefuseLoneSurrogateEscapes proves the Section 1.6\n12|// decoder rule at every production entry that reads a wire body: a lone\n13|// \\ud800-style escape is refused before any member is trusted, so the\n14|// silent U+FFFD replacement encoding/json would perform is never\n15|// observed. The bodies below are otherwise uninterpretable on purpose:\n16|// the gate runs before member validation, so refusal must not depend on\n17|// the members being present.\n18|func TestProductionEntriesRefuseLoneSurrogateEscapes(t *testing.T) {\n19|\tlone := []byte(`{\"note\":\"ab\\ud800cd\"}`)\n20|\trows := []struct {\n21|\t\tname string\n22|\t\terr  error\n23|\t}{\n24|\t\t{\"manifest\", DecodeManifest(lone)},\n25|\t\t{\"probe\", DecodeProbe(lone)},\n26|\t\t{\"identity\", CheckIdentity(lone, \"antigravity\")},\n27|\t\t{\"identify result\", DecodeIdentifyResult(lone, \"antigravity\")},\n28|\t\t{\"quiesce\", quiesceErr(lone)},\n29|\t\t{\"spawn plan\", DecodeSpawnPlan(lone, \"codex\", ProfileYOLO, scalar.PlatformLinux)},\n30|\t\t{\"response envelope\", func() error {\n31|\t\t\t_, err := DecodeResponse(successFrame(t, testRequestID, `{\"provider_id\":\"pi\",\"tag\":\"a\\ud800b\"}`), mustUUIDv7(t, testRequestID))\n32|\t\t\treturn err\n33|\t\t}()},\n34|\t}\n35|\tfor _, row := range rows {\n36|\t\tt.Run(row.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n37|\t\t\trequireFrameRefusal(t, row.err, \"\", \"lone surrogate escape\")\n38|\t\t})\n39|\t}\n40|\tt.Run(\"status outcome\", func(t *testing.T) {\n41|\t\tbody := strings.Replace(string(preparedBody()), `\"prepared\"`, `\"pre\\ud800pared\"`, 1)\n42|\t\t_, err := DecodeStatusOutcome([]byte(body), testStatusIDs())\n43|\t\trequireIntegrityRefusal(t, err, \"status body is lone surrogate escape\", \"\")\n44|\t})\n45|}\n46|\n47|// surrogateVectors is the shared corpus the local gate and the shared\n48|// canonicaljson decoder must agree on. Every vector is a top-level\n49|// object so both decodeStrictObject and canonicaljson.Canonicalize can\n50|// judge it. wantRefuse true means both must refuse; false means both\n51|// must accept. The member-name vector is fixed up in the test below,\n52|// where the escape stays visible next to its assertion.\n53|var surrogateVectors = []struct {\n54|\tname       string\n55|\tbody       string\n56|\twantRefuse bool\n57|}{\n58|\t{\"lone high surrogate in value\", `{\"a\":\"\\ud800\"}`, true},\n59|\t{\"lone low surrogate in value\", `{\"a\":\"\\udc00\"}`, true},\n60|\t{\"uppercase lone high surrogate\", `{\"a\":\"\\uD800\"}`, true},\n61|\t{\"high surrogate before literal\", `{\"a\":\"\\ud800A\"}`, true},\n62|\t{\"high surrogate before non-low escape\", `{\"a\":\"\\ud800\\u0041\"}`, true},\n63|\t{\"high surrogate at string end\", `{\"a\":\"x\\udbff\"}`, true},\n64|\t{\"lone surrogate in member name\", \"\", true},\n65|\t{\"lone surrogate in nested object\", `{\"a\":{\"b\":\"\\ud800\"}}`, true},\n66|\t{\"lone surrogate in array element\", `{\"a\":[\"\\ud800\"]}`, true},\n67|\t{\"valid surrogate pair decodes\", `{\"a\":\"\\ud83d\\ude00\"}`, false},\n68|\t{\"plain escape decodes\", `{\"a\":\"\\u0041\"}`, false},\n69|\t{\"escaped backslash before u is text\", `{\"a\":\"\\\\ud800\"}`, false},\n70|\t{\"plain string decodes\", `{\"a\":\"plain\"}`, false},\n71|}\n72|\n73|// TestSurrogateGateAgreesWithCanonicalJSON pins the local copy of the\n74|// shared surrogate rule to the canonical owner: on every shared vector\n75|// both implementations must reach the same verdict. A drift in either\n76|// direction (the gate admitting what canonicaljson refuses, or\n77|// refusing what it accepts) reddens here.\n78|func TestSurrogateGateAgreesWithCanonicalJSON(t *testing.T) {\n79|\tfor _, vector := range surrogateVectors {\n80|\t\tbody := vector.body\n81|\t\tif vector.name == \"lone surrogate in member name\" {\n82|\t\t\tbody = \"{\\\"\\\\ud800\\\":1}\"\n83|\t\t}\n84|\t\tt.Run(vector.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n85|\t\t\t_, canonicalErr := canonicaljson.Canonicalize([]byte(body))\n86|\t\t\t_, gateFault := decodeStrictObject([]byte(body))\n87|\t\t\tcanonicalRefuses := canonicalErr != nil\n88|\t\t\tgateRefuses := gateFault != nil\n89|\t\t\tif canonicalRefuses != vector.wantRefuse {\n90|\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"canonicaljson verdict = refuse:%v, want refuse:%v (err: %v)\", canonicalRefuses, vector.wantRefuse, canonicalErr)\n91|\t\t\t}\n92|\t\t\tif gateRefuses != vector.wantRefuse {\n93|\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"provhost gate verdict = refuse:%v, want refuse:%v (fault: %+v)\", gateRefuses, vector.wantRefuse, gateFault)\n94|\t\t\t}\n95|\t\t})\n96|\t}\n97|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c441","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070ab-e5ec-77e1-8b81-6518e8a08211"},"sequence":123,"recorded_at":1780531400000241,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34"},"call_id":"call_01a070ac26d17a52bedad6b0d143c737","text":"Read text file `internal/provider/doc.go`.\n1|// Package provider implements the M0 trusted provider plugin host boundary\n2|// of pinned specification Section 7.1: deterministic executable discovery,\n3|// trust recording, and substitution detection.\n4|//\n5|// The actor every Section 7.1 RFC 2119 keyword governs here is the ax host\n6|// (this repository). The provider plugin is a child process this package\n7|// never builds, probes, or executes: Discover takes no probe or execution\n8|// callback, so the Section 7.1 duplicate refusal structurally happens before\n9|// any probe or execution. Later leaves own the wire protocol (Section 7.2),\n10|// manifests and capabilities (Sections 7.3-7.7, 8), and process lifecycle.\n11|//\n12|// This package is an internal M0 host boundary, not a public stable plugin\n13|// SDK: Section 7.1 states that M0 MUST NOT advertise a public stable plugin\n14|// SDK, so no exported symbol offers plugin authorship, version negotiation,\n15|// or compatibility promises. The package lives under internal/ and declares\n16|// no stability contract.\n17|//\n18|// Discovery consumes only operator configuration (providers.plugin_dirs in\n19|// listed order, built-in adapters, then PATH when allow_path_plugins is\n20|// true) and the host filesystem. Everything crossing that boundary is\n21|// treated as attacker-influenced: a failed or partial read is reported as a\n22|// failure, never as an absence that a fallback could treat as satisfied.\n23|//\n24|// The package mutates no durable state of its own. Trust returns a\n25|// TrustRecord value the host persists; Verify rechecks that receipt against\n26|// freshly read filesystem facts. There is therefore no crash or idempotency\n27|// surface inside this package: repeated Discover calls over an unchanged\n28|// tree return identical candidates, and Verify is a pure recheck.\n29|//\n30|// A discovered candidate carries no availability, status, or capability\n31|// claim. Discovery answers which executable serves a provider ID; whether\n32|// any capability is available is decided by the probe plane (a later leaf),\n33|// never advertised here.\n34|//\n35|// Wire composition contract: Error Detail deliberately names provider\n36|// IDs, paths, and trust dimensions in human text, and Error() appends\n37|// the filesystem cause verbatim for errors.Is and errors.As. That\n38|// rendering must never become a wire message as-is:\n39|// axerror.refuseCausalLeak refuses any message reproducing a local\n40|// cause, so the future cmd/ax lift of a discovery failure into a\n41|// Structured Error must rebuild the message (dynamic facts into the\n42|// Details map, cause stripped) rather than quoting Error(). No such\n43|// call site exists yet; this paragraph names the rule that lift must\n44|// follow.\n45|package provider","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
