{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c479","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070ab-e5ec-77e1-8b81-6518e8a08211"},"sequence":151,"recorded_at":1780531400000297,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1229eb00-c1b9-42f9-9a80-dbf339369c34"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"b0607329-53f7-41a3-9fb3-83a0b82ffd5c"},"task_id":"b0607329-53f7-41a3-9fb3-83a0b82ffd5c","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"b0607329-53f7-41a3-9fb3-83a0b82ffd5c","chunk":"Read text file `internal/provhost/doc.go`.\n1|// Package provhost implements the ax host side of the provider plugin\n2|// JSON-over-stdio protocol of pinned specification Section 7.2: one-frame\n3|// JSONL transport, deadlines, size limits, stdout/stderr separation,\n4|// operation dispatch, and status recovery.\n5|//\n6|// The actor every Section 7.2 RFC 2119 keyword governs here is the ax host\n7|// (this repository). The provider plugin is a child process this package\n8|// starts but never trusts: every byte it emits is attacker-influenced, so\n9|// every frame is validated before any member is used, and a different-major\n10|// payload is never parsed far enough to trust its error code, retryable\n11|// bit, details, or authority fields (Section 15.1).\n12|//\n13|// This package owns the envelope layer and the operation-layer\n14|// conformance decoders. Operation dispatch means the host maps an\n15|// operation name to one plugin invocation and refuses unknown names\n16|// locally without spawning; the conformance decoders validate what a\n17|// plugin returned: the closed manifest (7.3), probe and capability\n18|// gate (7.4, 8.3), profile mapping (7.7), quiescence proof (7.6),\n19|// SpawnPlan success bodies for launch and resume, the Provider\n20|// Identity Record and identify-session result (5.5), and the single\n21|// (operation, operation_id) mutation key (7.5). Full Section 7.5\n22|// request-body vocabularies beyond these surfaces, and the\n23|// materialize commit/rollback result bodies, cross this package\n24|// opaquely: a body must still be a JSON object, and the\n25|// materialize-status body this package interprets for recovery must\n26|// satisfy the state/nullability/identity rules Section 7.5 states\n27|// for ProviderTransactionStatus.\n28|//\n29|// A failed read is never an absence: a crashed, timed-out, oversized,\n30|// unparsable, or correlation-mismatched invocation fails with a local\n31|// Structured Error 1.0.0, never with a partial response the caller could\n32|// mistake for a result. Status recovery is fail-closed the same way: an\n33|// unknown or integrity-invalid transaction fails with integrity_failure\n34|// and must be quarantined rather than represented as a successful status.\n35|//\n36|// The package mutates no durable state of its own and keeps no cross-call\n37|// cache: Host starts one plugin process per operation, so successive calls\n38|// are separate processes observing durable state through the authority the\n39|// caller passes, which is the property the cross-process recovery cases\n40|// rest on. Retry decisions belong to the caller; every local failure this\n41|// package emits is non-retryable, because nothing observed at an unusable\n42|// frame licenses a safe-retry claim.\n43|//\n44|// Stated bounds: stderr capture is capped at 1 MiB (an implementation\n45|// resource bound, not a protocol limit); error details carry the failure\n46|// class, member names, and the observed version string only — never\n47|// environment or stderr content. Mutation receipts live in the\n48|// provider transaction document and the plugin, not in the host: the\n49|// host holds no cross-call cache, so cross-crash durability of\n50|// (operation, operation_id) results is the document's property, and\n51|// the host side pins the sole key shape, byte-identical retry bytes,\n52|// and the mismatch surface. The Section 8 provider/platform cell\n53|// values are evidence labels, not host behavior: the host pins the\n54|// fail-closed direction (conditional, unsupported, and unknown are\n55|// never usable) for every capability-by-status tuple rather than one\n56|// cell value. Section 8.2 store-exclusion roots and the remaining\n57|// Section 7.5 request vocabularies have no host production yet.\n58|//\n59|// Provider Protocol 3.0.0, which the v0.5.0 catalog pins alongside\n60|// 2.0.0, has no implementation owner in this package: this host\n61|// speaks major 2 only, classifies any recognizable 3.x envelope as\n62|// incompatible_protocol without trusting its payload, and carries no\n63|// Structured Error 1.3.0 binding or launch/resume descriptor carriage.\n64|// A v3 plugin is therefore refused loudly rather than misread as v2;\n65|// naming the v3 envelope owner is open work, not a silent scope.\n66|package provhost"}}}
